window.LyraDocsIndex = [{"title": "Getting started", "description": "Learn about the editions, check the requirements and prepare for your first contact with Lyra OS.", "url": "primeiros-passos.html", "text": "Lyra OS 1.1 is in development. Alpha and Beta images are test releases. Start in a virtual machine or on a dedicated computer and keep a copy of your files. Choose your edition Lyra OS is a system based on openSUSE Leap, with its own tools to organize the user and administration experience. The Lyra version is independent from the version of its base. Desktop: for personal computers. This guide follows the GNOME edition and the Vega control center. Server: for services and infrastructure, without a graphical environment. Administration uses the console, Vega CLI and Vega Web. Use the downloads section to choose the published image. The Desktop and Server cycles are independent; do not expect the same development stage in both editions. Check the requirements See the Desktop and Server requirements table before preparing the machine. Set aside a dedicated disk, UEFI boot and network access for updates. Try it before installing In a virtual machine, use UEFI firmware and a dedicated virtual disk. On the Desktop, the live session lets you check video, keyboard, mouse, audio and network before proceeding. Write down anything that does not work so you can include it in a problem report. The documented installation flow uses the entire disk. To prepare the media and review the disk precautions, follow Install Lyra OS . After the first login Confirm language, keyboard, time zone and network connection. Open Vega from the applications menu and check the system summary. Look up the available updates and read the changes before confirming. Set up a backup routine for your files on another device. Continue with Meet Vega or with the Server guide ."}, {"title": "Install Lyra OS", "description": "From ISO download to first boot: integrity, installation media and target disk.", "url": "instalacao.html", "text": "1. Prepare the computer Check the edition requirements , a network connection and a USB drive large enough for the ISO. A virtual machine with a dedicated disk also works for testing. A whole-disk installation erases the data on the selected disk. Back up before you start and check the model and capacity of the target. The USB drive will also be erased when the image is written. This guide covers the Desktop with GNOME. For the console installer and the storage limits of the other edition, see Install the Server . Do not use this procedure as instructions for dual boot or manual partitioning. 2. Download and verify the ISO Go to the website's downloads section . Download the ISO and the matching checksum file from the same release directory on SourceForge. On Linux, open a terminal in the folder containing both files. Example for the Desktop Alpha 7 image: sha256sum -c LyraOS-Desktop-1.1-alpha.7-x86_64.iso.sha256 The name must match the downloaded file exactly. Continue only if the check reports OK . If there is a mismatch, download the files again. The checksum verifies integrity; according to its release notes, Alpha 7 does not have a detached GPG signature for the ISO. 3. Write the media and boot in UEFI mode Open a disk image writing tool. In GNOME Disks, select the USB drive and use the option to restore a disk image. Select the ISO and double-check the target device before confirming. Copying the ISO file into a folder on the USB drive does not replace writing the image. Wait for the write to finish, eject the media and restart the computer. Open the firmware boot menu and select the media's UEFI entry. The key varies by manufacturer. In a virtual machine, attach the ISO as an optical drive and enable UEFI. Secure Boot is part of the flow planned by the project; record any boot failure for investigation. 4. Follow the Lyra Installer Wait for the live session and for the Lyra Installer to open. Fill in the language, keyboard, region and account options presented by the installer. Choose the disk dedicated to the installation and review the summary, especially the target and the storage changes. Confirm only after reviewing the data. Keep the computer on until the installation completes. Restart when prompted and remove the media to boot from the installed disk. The screens may change between candidates. The videos on the download page show earlier versions and serve as a visual reference. 5. Check the installed system Log in with the account you created, test the network and open Vega. Follow the first steps after installation . If the installer fails, write down the step and the message before restarting; see how to report the problem . Reference: Desktop 1.1 Alpha 7 release notes (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Lyra OS Server", "description": "Install the server edition and start managing it from the console with Vega CLI.", "url": "server.html", "text": "An edition for service administration Lyra OS Server is an edition without a graphical environment. It is administered through the console, Vega CLI and Vega Web. The 1.1 Beta 1.1 candidate uses the openSUSE Leap 16.1 base and keeps the functional scope of Beta 1. Storage in the Server 1.1 series: whole-disk UEFI installation, with GPT, an EFI partition and an ext4 root. RAID, LVM, Btrfs, Snapper, legacy BIOS and unattended installation are outside the documented flow for this candidate. Install the Server Check the requirements , prepare a dedicated disk and back up. The contents of the chosen disk will be erased. Download the Server image from the downloads section and verify the integrity files from the same release. Write the ISO and boot in UEFI mode. The installer is interactive and runs in the console. Enter the language, keyboard, time zone, hostname and account details requested. Review the chosen disk and confirm the installation only after checking the summary. When it finishes, restart from the installed disk and log in with the account you created. Prepare Ethernet with DHCP for the initial setup. Network adjustments, such as a static address, are made after installation. Manage with Vega CLI On a local interactive console or in an already configured SSH session, run as your user: vega Use the menus to check the system state and manage the available functions. Authentication is requested for each privileged operation. Check the first boot These commands query the system state without changing the configuration: cat /etc/os-release ip -brief address systemctl --failed Check the installed version, the address received by the network interface and any failed services. The initial policy described by the project allows SSH and Vega Web on port 9090/tcp; confirm the actual state of the firewall and services on your installation before remote access. Vega Web is intended for administration on the local network. See its documentation to configure HTTPS access and authentication: Vega Web guide . Keep a recovery copy Plan backups of the data and of the service configurations. This edition does not offer Snapper rollback or automatic downgrade of an installation on the new base. See updates and troubleshooting . References: Server Beta 1.1 release notes and Server architecture (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Apps and updates", "description": "Manage packages and understand the difference between updating the system and upgrading to a new version.", "url": "atualizacoes.html", "text": "Install apps with Vega Open Vega and go to the software area. Search for the app, check its source and review the operation before confirming. Vega manages RPM packages via Zypper and Flatpak apps when that support is available. RPMs are part of the distribution's package system. Flatpaks use their own repositories and runtimes. The same app may appear from more than one source; choose the one you intend to keep to avoid duplicate installations. Update the installed system Connect the computer to the network and to a power source. Open the Vega updates area and refresh the list of available updates. Read the summary and confirm the changes you want. Wait for the result. Restart if the tool asks you to or if an update needs a new boot to take effect. If you prefer the terminal, the update flow for RPM packages is: sudo zypper refresh sudo zypper update The first command refreshes the repository metadata; the second presents the updates for confirmation. They do not update Flatpak apps. Do not run another package operation through Vega at the same time. Take care of package sources Keep the repositories that match the base of your installation. Do not add repositories from another Leap version to get a single package. If an unknown key or an unexpected change of source appears, check the project's announcement before accepting. Updating is not upgrading to a new version Changing the base requires a specific procedure. Updating packages does not mean you can migrate between openSUSE Leap versions or between Lyra candidates. Wait for a documented migration path for your source and target. The Alpha 7 notes state that migrating existing installations from the Leap 16.0 base to 16.1 depends on specific validation. Do not change the repository addresses or use a distribution upgrade as a shortcut. Backup and recovery Keep your important files on another device before major changes. Snapshots, when configured, help recover the system, but they do not replace an independent copy of the data. Server 1.1 uses ext4 and does not offer the Desktop's Snapper rollback flow. References: management with Vega and Alpha 7 migration limits (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "description": "Investigate boot, network and update failures and prepare a useful report for the project.", "url": "solucao-de-problemas.html", "text": "The media does not boot Check the ISO checksum as described in the installation guide . Confirm that the image was written to the USB drive and that the firmware is booting from the correct UEFI entry. In a VM, check UEFI, memory and that the ISO is attached to the optical drive. Record the message shown, the machine model and the Secure Boot state. No network connection Check the cable or the Wi-Fi connection in the settings. On the Server, confirm that the network provides DHCP. To look up the interface addresses: ip -brief address If there is an address but the repositories do not respond, note whether the error mentions DNS, connection or certificate. Also check the system date and time. This information helps distinguish network failures from an unavailable repository. An update failed Wait for other package operations to finish and try again. Do not delete lock files or force the package manager to quit. Copy the error message and the name of the repository involved. Read the guidance on package sources before accepting a new key, removing repositories or changing the base. Gather system information To identify the version, the kernel and failed services, run: cat /etc/os-release uname -r systemctl --failed If your image includes lyra-report , run it as a regular user: lyra-report The tool shows the collected data for review before creating a local file and does not upload anything automatically. If the command is not available, use the basic information above; you do not need to install development components to report a failure. Report a reproducible problem Open an issue in the corresponding repository: Desktop , Server or Vega . Search for a similar report first. Include the information below: Lyra OS edition and version: ISO name, if the failure happened during installation: Computer model or VM configuration: UEFI and Secure Boot state: Steps to reproduce: Expected result: Observed result and error message: Review screenshots and logs before publishing. Remove passwords, tokens, keys and personal data, even when the tool applies anonymization. Share only the files needed to explain the problem. Reference: diagnostics with lyra-report ."}, {"title": "Requirements and technical terms", "description": "Look up the hardware requirements and the meaning of the terms used in the guides.", "url": "referencia.html", "text": "Check the requirements The values below are provisional and follow the requirements published on the website. Apps and services may need more resources. Minimum and recommended configuration Resource Desktop Server Minimum processor x86_64, 2 cores x86_64, 1 core Minimum memory 4 GiB 2 GiB Minimum disk 24 GiB 8 GiB Recommended 4 cores, 8 GiB of RAM, 40 GiB SSD or larger 2 or more cores, 4 GiB of RAM or more, 20 GiB SSD or larger Set up UEFI boot. On the Desktop, the minimum screen resolution is 1024 × 768, with Full HD recommended. On the Server, prepare an Ethernet connection with DHCP. Make sure you have internet access to get updates. Terms used in these guides ISO and live session An ISO is the image used to create the boot media. The live session runs the system from that media before installation. UEFI and Secure Boot UEFI is the firmware used in the documented boot flow. Secure Boot verifies the signatures of boot components according to the firmware's trust configuration. RPM and Zypper RPM is the system's package format. Zypper manages these packages and their repositories. Flatpak An app distribution format and tool with its own repositories and runtimes. Snapshot and backup A snapshot records a state of the file system. A backup is a copy of the data for recovery; an independent copy also protects against failure of the original disk. SHA-256 checksum A value used to check whether a file matches the expected content. It must be obtained from the release that corresponds to the image. Identify your installation cat /etc/os-release uname -r The first command shows the system identification. The second reports the running kernel. Also include the ISO name when reporting installation problems. References: requirements published on the website , Desktop Alpha 7 and Server Beta 1.1 (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Meet Vega", "description": "Find software, network, hardware and service settings in the control center.", "url": "vega.html", "text": "Open the control center Vega brings together Lyra OS administration tasks. On the Desktop, look for Vega in the applications menu and open it with your regular account. Administrative authentication is requested when an operation needs authorization. GNOME Settings remains available for desktop preferences. Vega complements those options with management of packages, services and other system features. Where to find each task Dashboard: summary of the system's health and shortcuts. Software: packages, Flatpak apps, repositories and updates. Hardware and system: device inventory, kernel, storage, date and time. Network: connections, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, firewall, VPN and proxy, depending on the available components. Administration: users, services, logs and processes. Backup: features that depend on the installed tools and storage configuration. Screen names and availability may vary with the installed version. Optional features may appear unavailable when a dependency is missing. Before confirming an operation Read the action summary and check what will be installed, removed or changed. Wait for the operation to finish before starting another package management task. If an error occurs, record the full message and the chosen action. To manage programs and keep the system up to date, follow Apps and updates . Vega in the terminal On the Server with Vega CLI installed, open an interactive terminal with your account and run: vega The terminal interface shares the administration service with the other Vega interfaces. Queries do not require running the whole application as root; privileged actions request authentication. See also the Server guide . References: Vega documentation and Vega CLI ."}, {"title": "Versions and support", "description": "Understand Alpha, Beta, RC, Lyra's version numbers and how they relate to the openSUSE base.", "url": "versoes.html", "text": "Lyra has its own version numbers The project uses the MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH format: a product generation, a compatible evolution within that generation and a maintenance revision. The 1.1 line is in development. The openSUSE Leap base is identified separately. A change of base does not automatically determine the Lyra OS version number. Alpha, Beta and RC Alpha: development candidate for testing integration and finding problems. Beta: validation and stabilization stage, still before the final release. RC: release candidate, subject to approval against the quality criteria. Identifiers such as 1.1-alpha.7 and 1.1-beta.1.1 distinguish the candidates. Desktop and Server have independent cycles. Moving to the next stage depends on testing and on resolving blockers, not just on a date. Check the notes for your image Always consult the release that corresponds to the file you downloaded. These are the documents for the images referenced in this edition of the guides: Desktop 1.1 Alpha 7 : move of the base to Leap 16.1 and installation and upgrade limits. Server 1.1 Beta 1.1 : base revision preserving the scope of Beta 1. Community support Lyra OS is an independent project and version 1.1 will have community support. No contractual timeframe or end-of-support date is promised without a formal policy. Support for the openSUSE base and the cycle of a Lyra version are separate matters. To ask for help, describe the version and the situation following the problem reporting guide . For details of the policy maintained on the website, see the versioning document (in Portuguese)."}, {"title": "Contribute to the documentation", "description": "Suggest corrections, report questions and help improve the guides for other people.", "url": "contribuir.html", "text": "Found a gap? The documentation follows the evolution of Lyra OS. A confusing step, a different message in your version or a missing topic are good opportunities to contribute. Open the website repository and use the issues area to propose a correction. Include the page, the passage, the Lyra version you used and the result you observed. For system defects, use the repositories listed in the troubleshooting guide . Propose a change Fork the website repository on GitHub and create a branch for the change. Edit the article content in docs/content/ . The catalog.json file organizes titles, descriptions and categories. Run python3 scripts/build-docs.py at the project root to generate the pages and the search index. Check the navigation, the instructions and the layout on a small screen. Open a pull request with the correction and explain which version you checked the procedure on. Write for the people who will use the system Start with the goal and the requirements of the procedure. Explain what commands do before asking people to run them. Highlight operations that erase data or change storage. Keep released features separate from plans and features in development. Include a reference to the release notes or to the component's documentation. Translate and update translations Portuguese is the source language of the guides. The English, Spanish and Italian versions live in docs/content/en/ , docs/content/es/ and docs/content/it/ , each with its own catalog.json . Keep the same file name, the same h2 heading IDs and the commands unchanged. When revising a guide in Portuguese, also update the translations or leave them flagged as outdated. In the translation catalog, source_reviewed records the review date of the Portuguese original used as the basis. When the original is newer, the translated page shows a notice linking to the Portuguese version."}];
